# Maths: written methods laid out as on paper. One line each: the method and the sum. The painter works every digit; the brain writes none, so the working cannot be
# wrong and the answer is never on the page until the learner writes it. Blank boxes are the learner's. `pnpm check` plays and judges each scene.
# Each scene stays up for as long as reading its working takes (the reading rule of `pnpm timeline`, fb-023): the wait after a block is that time. A `math` block holds
# the pen until it has arrived, so the wait starts when the working is on the page. The scene that steps on a tap is last: no wait is long enough for a tap.

scene stack
say a1 | Six lots of 12, added in a column. Start with the ones.
math m1 column | 12+12+12+12+12+12
set m1.ans-ones mark=box
say near=m1.r3:left | a 12
wait 8s

scene stack
say a2 | Take 278 from 503. The ones need an exchange first.
math m2 column | 503 - 278
wait 8s

scene stack
say a3 | Forty-seven, six times.
math m3 times | 47 × 6
hit m3.tens pulse
wait 6s

scene stack
say a4 | Pull 347 apart, place by place.
math m4 split | 347
wait 6.5s

scene stack
say a5 | The whole is 10, one part is 4. What is the other part?
math m5 bond | 10 = 4 + ?
wait 6.6s

scene stack
say a6 | Line the decimal points up, then add.
math m6 column | 3.4 + 12.75
wait 8s

scene stack
say a8 | Three quarters of a bar.
math m8 bar | 3/4
wait 4.2s

scene stack
say a9 | Twelve shared into three groups.
math m9 groups | 12 ÷ 3
wait 4.2s

scene stack
say a10 | Three rows of four.
math m10 array | 3 × 4
wait 4s

scene stack
say a11 | Seven, and five more.
math m11 line | 7 + 5
wait 4s

scene stack
say a7 | Here it is worked, a step at a time. Tap for the next one.
math m7 column worked steps | 346 + 285
